From: Mirdispo contributors
Subject: Hold each encoder to its own numbers

The rate the picture is held to, and the buffer the rate control answers to,
were found by watching one encoder on one link. They were then applied to
every encoder, which is how a figure that suits one comes to be imposed on
another that it does not suit, and on machines using a third that nobody
watched at all.

Encoders do not share these numbers. They answer to different rate controls,
reach a given picture at different costs, and come apart differently when
asked for more than they can give. A rate one of them keeps steadily is one
another overshoots in bursts, and a buffer that lets one borrow enough for a
hard frame is cramped or wasteful for the next.

Each now has its own line, and is tuned by changing that line. The buffer is
written in frames, because that is what it comes to whichever unit an
encoder asks for it in, and is turned into milliseconds or kilobits where
the encoder is set up. Zero leaves an encoder at the receiver's announced
maximum and its own default buffer, which is what the two that were never
measured now get, rather than numbers found by watching something else.

What is in force is said in the log, by encoder name, so a session shows
which numbers it ran under.

